WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.140
The adult cortex is divided into areas.

2
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.440
The embryo's cortex shows no borders at all.

3
00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:11.620
So where do the areas come from?

4
00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:16.310
The cortex is the sheet of neurons on the surface of the forebrain.

5
00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:23.183
In 1909, Korbinian Brodmann divided the human cortex into areas by how its cells

6
00:00:23.183 --> 00:00:24.343
were packed and layered.

7
00:00:25.383 --> 00:00:28.103
Many of his borders match borders of function.

8
00:00:28.803 --> 00:00:34.803
His area 17 is primary visual cortex, the first cortical stop for signals from the

9
00:00:34.803 --> 00:00:35.803
eyes.

10
00:00:36.117 --> 00:00:37.737
This lesson works in the mouse.

11
00:00:38.617 --> 00:00:39.937
Here is the map we will use throughout.

12
00:00:41.017 --> 00:00:44.377
Front to back runs from the nose end to the back of the head.

13
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.690
The medial edge runs along the midline.

14
00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:50.170
The lateral edge is the outer side.

15
00:00:51.183 --> 00:00:53.043
Motor cortex sits at the front.

16
00:00:54.717 --> 00:00:57.457
Somatosensory cortex, the touch area, sits in the middle.

17
00:00:58.697 --> 00:01:00.917
Inside it lies the barrel field, the whisker map.

18
00:01:01.917 --> 00:01:05.417
Auditory cortex sits on the lateral side, toward the back.

19
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.340
Visual cortex sits at the back.

20
00:01:09.283 --> 00:01:13.083
We will call motor and somatosensory the front-and-middle areas.

21
00:01:14.323 --> 00:01:15.343
Visual is the back area.

22
00:01:16.317 --> 00:01:20.797
The cortex grows from a smooth sheet of progenitors, cells that divide to make

23
00:01:20.797 --> 00:01:21.797
neurons.

24
00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:26.330
The sheet that will hold the whisker map and the visual map looks the same from

25
00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:27.330
front to back.

26
00:01:27.933 --> 00:01:30.833
Making the areas from it is called arealization.

27
00:01:31.883 --> 00:01:37.503
Signalling centres at the edges of the sheet are active by about embryonic day 9.5.

28
00:01:38.363 --> 00:01:40.803
They keep signalling as the first neurons are made.

29
00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:47.087
Progenitors make the cortex's neurons from about embryonic day 11 to 17.

30
00:01:48.217 --> 00:01:52.717
The thalamus is the relay station for signals from the eyes, ears and skin.

31
00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:58.710
Which comes first: the neurons of the cortex, or the thalamic axons that reach them?

32
00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:06.550
The first neurons are born first.

33
00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:13.790
Thalamic axons reach the developing cortex around embryonic day 14 to 15, and wait

34
00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:15.210
in the layer just under it.

35
00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:17.610
More neurons are still being made then.

36
00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:21.830
The axons grow into the cortex itself later, around birth.

37
00:02:22.833 --> 00:02:27.353
Barrels can be seen in the first postnatal week, around postnatal day 4.

38
00:02:28.333 --> 00:02:32.893
So the sheet is already patterned and making neurons before the thalamic axons

39
00:02:32.893 --> 00:02:33.893
arrive.

40
00:02:34.283 --> 00:02:37.223
Where do the borders come from, in a sheet that shows none?

41
00:02:38.233 --> 00:02:42.613
What tells a progenitor where it sits, and can that be changed so a border moves?

42
00:02:43.666 --> 00:02:47.826
And what does input from the thalamus add, if the sheet has already been marked?

43
00:02:48.816 --> 00:02:53.936
The takeaway: the areas are real, and the embryonic sheet shows no borders for them.

44
00:02:54.776 --> 00:02:57.136
The next chapters ask what draws them, and when.
